1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:01,931
What happens when the machines

2
00:00:01,931 --> 00:00:03,200
can't read your label?

3
00:00:03,950 --> 00:00:05,822
It got wet in the rain.

4
00:00:06,722 --> 00:00:08,930
The box is pulled out of the flow.

5
00:00:09,230 --> 00:00:11,102
Now what?

6
00:00:12,252 --> 00:00:14,023
Now, a photo of the label

7
00:00:14,023 --> 00:00:15,228
goes to a person.

8
00:00:15,528 --> 00:00:17,400
A real one.

9
00:00:17,700 --> 00:00:19,865
When UPS showed how this works, that

10
00:00:19,865 --> 00:00:21,729
person had about ten seconds to

11
00:00:21,729 --> 00:00:23,172
figure out the ZIP code.

12
00:00:23,572 --> 00:00:25,296
If even that fails, someone

13
00:00:25,296 --> 00:00:26,764
checks the box by hand.

14
00:00:27,614 --> 00:00:29,270
Three seconds left.

15
00:00:29,570 --> 00:00:31,442
Got it.

16
00:00:32,742 --> 00:00:35,526
And sometimes the problem isn't one label.

17
00:00:36,426 --> 00:00:39,149
On the night of April second, 2025, with

18
00:00:39,149 --> 00:00:41,804
severe storms on the way, UPS suspended

19
00:00:41,804 --> 00:00:44,459
its Next Day Air sort in Louisville and

20
00:00:44,459 --> 00:00:46,842
canceled incoming domestic flights.

21
00:00:47,142 --> 00:00:49,251
When the center of the network stops,

22
00:00:49,251 --> 00:00:50,790
the whole network feels it.

23
00:00:52,190 --> 00:00:54,134
Tonight, the storm passes.

24
00:00:55,540 --> 00:00:57,724
ZERO's box makes its plane.
